# Absolute mouse: the absolute_mouse CircuitPython library (MIT, David Glaude
# and Neradoc), the descriptor the RP2040 HID board presents, with its Report
# ID removed. Roadie tried a 7-byte layout on the board (2a2c9b8) and
# reverted it (336e425); this is the one that works.
#
# Input report, 6 bytes:  [buttons, x_lo, x_hi, y_lo, y_hi, wheel]
#   buttons: bit 0 left, 1 right, 2 middle, 3-4 back/forward
#   x, y:    0-32767 across the target's pointer space
#   wheel:   signed, relative
05 01        # Usage Page (Generic Desktop)
09 02        # Usage (Mouse)
A1 01        # Collection (Application)
09 01        #   Usage (Pointer)
A1 00        #   Collection (Physical)
05 09        #     Usage Page (Button)
19 01        #     Usage Minimum (1)
29 05        #     Usage Maximum (5)
15 00        #     Logical Minimum (0)
25 01        #     Logical Maximum (1)
95 05        #     Report Count (5)
75 01        #     Report Size (1)
81 02        #     Input (Data,Var,Abs)        buttons
75 03        #     Report Size (3)
95 01        #     Report Count (1)
81 03        #     Input (Const)               padding
05 01        #     Usage Page (Generic Desktop)
09 30        #     Usage (X)
09 31        #     Usage (Y)
15 00        #     Logical Minimum (0)
26 FF 7F     #     Logical Maximum (32767)
75 10        #     Report Size (16)
95 02        #     Report Count (2)
81 02        #     Input (Data,Var,Abs)        x, y
09 38        #     Usage (Wheel)
15 81        #     Logical Minimum (-127)
25 7F        #     Logical Maximum (127)
75 08        #     Report Size (8)
95 01        #     Report Count (1)
81 06        #     Input (Data,Var,Rel)        wheel
C0           #   End Collection
C0           # End Collection
